<TopicPage slug="subjects/nuclear-medicine-technology/foundational/radiopharmaceuticals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Nuclear medicine turns the body into its own light source: inject a molecule carrying a
    radioactive atom, let physiology deliver it, and photograph where it goes. The discipline's
    foundation is the radiopharmaceutical — isotope plus courier molecule — and the principle that
    the image shows <strong>function</strong>, not just structure.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="isotope → courier → physiology → image" widget="equationStep" config={localisationCfg} />

  <h2>Why Tc-99m runs the field</h2>
  <p>
    Technetium-99m hits a rare sweet spot: a 6-hour half-life (long enough to image, short enough
    to vanish), a clean 140 keV gamma emission (ideal for cameras, no particle radiation), and
    versatile chemistry for binding to couriers. Daily "milking" of a molybdenum generator — the
    parent decaying into Tc-99m — supplies the department like a dairy.
  </p>

  <h2>Couriers decide the picture</h2>
  <p>
    The same isotope images bone, heart, thyroid or kidneys purely by the molecule it rides:
    phosphonates bind where bone remodels; sestamibi tracks heart-muscle perfusion; pertechnetate
    is trapped by thyroid tissue. Choosing the courier is choosing the question the scan will
    answer — the nuclear pharmacist's real craft.
  </p>

  <h2>Dose logic differs from X-ray work</h2>
  <p>
    In nuclear medicine the patient <em>is</em> the radiation source for hours: dose depends on the
    isotope's half-life, the radiation type, and how fast the body clears it (hence hydration and
    bathroom discipline after scans). The ALARA arithmetic works in reverse of X-ray imaging —
    minimising time in the room matters less than picking the right tracer and dose.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NMTCB content outline; Mettler &amp; Guiberteau, <em>Essentials of Nuclear Medicine Imaging</em>; Christian &amp; Waterstram-Rich, <em>Nuclear Medicine and PET/CT</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
